## `ApaClient` Requirements
Prefer this public shape unless the user asks for something else:

```csharp
public class ApaClient
{
    private readonly HttpClient _http;

    public string AccessToken { get; set; } = string.Empty;

    public ApaClient(HttpClient http)
    {
        _http = http;
    }

    public Task<T> SendAsync<T>(string operationName, string query, object? variables);
    public Task<T[]> SendBatchAsync<T>(IEnumerable<GraphQlRequest> requests);
}
```

Behavior rules:
- `SendAsync<T>` should wrap a single request into a one-item batch.
- `SendBatchAsync<T>` should send an array payload.
- Deserialize responses as `GraphQlResponse<T>[]`.
- If any response contains GraphQL errors, throw an exception with the returned messages.
- If the HTTP response is not successful, throw an exception.
- Attach the bearer token on every request.
- Keep the client focused on transport and serialization only.

---

## Query File Rules
- Put each query in its own `.graphql` file.
- Name files after the operation they contain.
- Prefer loading query text from disk or embedded resources.
- Keep C# free of large inline GraphQL strings when working in `ConnectAPA`.

---

## Starter Query
Use this as the default test query when needed.

### `ViewerQuery.graphql`
```graphql
query ViewerQuery {
  viewer {
    id
    __typename
    ... on Member {
      firstName
      lastName
      emailAddress
      leagues(current: true) {
        id
        name
        slug
      }
    }
  }
}
```

---

## Summary Rules
- Use `HttpClient`.
- Use `System.Text.Json`.
- Use `https://gql.poolplayers.com/graphql`.
- Use batched GraphQL payloads only.
- Use bearer token auth on every request.
- Load queries from `.graphql` files.
- Do not use `GraphQL.Client`.
- Do not use `Newtonsoft.Json`.
- Do not add SQL or EF logic to `ConnectAPA`.
